  "userPrompt": "This is another touch email for Wired CIO, a Microsoft Solutions Partner in Chicago working with small and midsize businesses to get better use of their Microsoft ecosystem  by offering ongoing support as a partner. We called this person and never reached them.\n\nThis is variant A of an A/B test. It takes the ownership angle, asking whether this is theirs to look after. Variant B takes the I tried calling angle. Keep A on its own angle but in the same easy, spoken tone as B.\n\nReference only:\nFirst name: {{contact.name}}\nCompany: {{account.name}}\nSolution interest: {{contact.Lead Solution Area}}\nProduct line: {{contact.Product Line}}\n\nOUTPUT EXACTLY THIS, two paragraphs, filling the three slots:\n\nHi {{contact.first_name}}, [[OPEN]]. I am with Wired CIO, a Microsoft Solutions Partner here in Chicago, and we spend most of our time on [[AREA]].\n\n[[CLOSE]] And if it is not your side of the house, happy to be pointed the right way.<br><br>\n\n[[OPEN]] choose by the FIRST LETTER of {{account.name}}:\n  A to I -> just reaching out once more to see if this is on your plate\n  J to R -> circling back one more time in case this one sits with you\n  S to Z -> trying you once more in case this is something you look after\n  anything else -> just reaching out once more to see if this is on your plate\n\n[[CLOSE]] choose by the LAST LETTER of {{account.name}}:\n  a to m -> If it is worth going a bit deeper, I can keep it to 15 minutes.\n  n to z -> Worth a quick chat to see if it is worth going deeper?\n  anything else -> If it is worth going a bit deeper, I can keep it to 15 minutes.\n\n[[AREA]] this is cold or warm outreach and we do NOT know what they run. Stay broad. Use EXACTLY ONE of these five phrases, spelled exactly:\n  1. their Microsoft 365 environment\n  2. their Azure and data platform\n  3. their Dynamics and business applications\n  4. their Microsoft licensing\n  5. their Microsoft environment\nChoose from the notes first, then solution interest, then product line. Email, Teams, SharePoint, Copilot or productivity signals -> 1. Cloud, infrastructure, migration, reporting, analytics or AI -> 2. ERP, CRM, finance, operations -> 3. Seats, renewals, agreements, resellers, CSP -> 4. Thin, empty, Not Applicable, or any doubt at all -> 5. When unsure always choose 5.\nNEVER name a specific product, edition, plan, tier or SKU anywhere. These five phrases are the only product wording allowed.\n",
